      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sorry about the goblin distractions! I'll try to keep it straightforward from now on.\n\nThe steady hum you're experiencing is more likely to be caused by a faulty contactor or a malfunctioning relay. These components control the flow of electricity to the compressor and fan motor, and if they're not working properly, it can cause the compressor to hum or whine, even if the blades aren't spinning.\n\nAs for testing the capacitor, it's a relatively simple process. To test a capacitor, you'll need a multimeter. Set the multimeter to the \"diode test\" function (usually denoted by a diode symbol on the meter) and touch the leads to the capacitor's terminals. If the capacitor is good, the multimeter will show a reading of around 10-20 volts. If the reading is zero or very low, the capacitor is likely faulty.\n\nHowever, be careful when testing the capacitor, as it can be a shock hazard if you touch the terminals incorrectly. Make sure to follow proper safety precautions, such as turning off the power to the unit at the circuit breaker or fuse box before testing.\n\nIt's also worth noting that if the capacitor is blown, it's likely to be a relatively expensive replacement,",
